<section class="panel" aria-label="2021 math first-party subtraction algorithm comparison">
<h2>Warm-up — Digits that Disappear</h2><p>Compare <strong>300 + 20</strong> with <strong>200 + 120</strong>. They are equal. What does a crossed-out 3 written as 2 in the hundreds place mean? Why is a 12 written above the tens place equivalent to 120?</p>
<h2>Activity 1 — Andre versus Clare</h2><p>Both methods subtract <strong>528 − 271</strong>.</p>
<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(245px,1fr));gap:12px">
<div style="border:1px solid #8daeb7;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><strong>Andre — expanded values</strong><p>500 + 20 + 8 → 400 + 120 + 8</p><p>Remove 200 + 70 + 1</p><p>Remain 200 + 50 + 7 = 257</p></div>
<div style="border:1px solid #8daeb7;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><strong>Clare — compact place values</strong><p>5 hundreds and 2 tens become 4 hundreds and 12 tens; 8 ones remain.</p><p>Subtract 2 hundreds, 7 tens, 1 one.</p><p>Result: 2 hundreds, 5 tens and 7 ones = 257</p></div></div>
<p>Teacher: compare which method records a partial-sums total and which directly records the final digits. The original pictured publisher algorithms are not reused here.</p>
<h2>Activity 2 — Try Clare's Algorithm</h2><p>Study the source worked example <strong>538 − 156 = 382</strong>. Then compute four source differences, showing decomposition:</p>
<ol><li>691 − 358</li><li>926 − 584</li><li>317 − 182</li><li>492 − 325</li></ol>
<h2>Teacher review and synthesis</h2><p>Use MLR8 'First, I... because...' and 'Then, I... because...' with a partner. Explain how a compact digit 4 in the hundreds place can mean 400, and 12 in the tens place can mean 120. The new Edulytics Cool-down is <strong>804 − 257</strong>, not a reproduced publisher prompt.</p>
